<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Recycling for Our World</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Composting Is Easy.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Assist peers in following multi-step school procedures. Concept: Explain the steps to solve a problem using practical examples.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can assist peers with recycling procedures by pointing to visuals (e.g., points to the recycling bin and says, "This is for paper."). Can explain steps for solving recycling problems by using practical examples, gestures, and peer collaboration (e.g., "First, sort your trash." and ask, "Can you sort the paper?"). Can simplify steps for recycling using keywords and visuals (e.g., points to each bin and says, "Paper here, plastic here").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows three labeled recycling bins (paper, plastic, compost) and holds up different items (e.g., an empty plastic bottle, a used paper, an apple core). Students quickly say where each item goes: "Plastic bin!", "Paper bin!", "Compost!" This activates prior knowledge of sorting trash and sets the context for our "Recycling for Our World" scenario.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models explaining a simple school procedure: "How to wash your hands." The teacher uses gestures, simple present tense verbs (e.g., "First, turn on the water. Then, get soap."), and points to visual aids (pictures of steps). The teacher emphasizes clear pronunciation of /r/ in "wash" and /s/ in "soap." Teacher models asking clarifying questions like, "Is it clear?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the modeled explanation. <br>1. "What do I do first when I wash my hands?" (Students respond: "Turn on the water.") <br>2. "Do I dry my hands before I get soap?" (Students respond: "No!") The teacher confirms, "Right, you get soap first."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Recycling for Our World".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a large visual poster titled "Our Classroom Recycling Data Chart Project: Steps to Help the Environment." The poster includes simple, numbered instructions for the project (e.g., "1. Sort your trash. 2. Collect data every day. 3. Write 'paper' or 'plastic' on the chart. 4. Count the items every week."). The instructions use keywords like 'sort', 'collect', 'chart', 'paper', 'plastic', 'waste', 'environment'. The genre is "instructions," the register is "informative and simple," and the format is "visual instructions with keywords."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding and interpreting simple multi-step instructions for a school project related to recycling.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> The teacher asks, "What is our new project about?" (Expected answer: "Recycling data chart.") "What do we want to help?" (Expected answer: "The environment.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> The teacher reads statements based on the poster. "We collect data about clothes." (False) "We count items every day." (False, every week). "We use the chart for paper and plastic." (True).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice mediating information using present simple, modal verbs (can/could), 'there is/are', and target vocabulary (plastic, paper, bin, sort, classify, help, save, environment, easy, waste). Focus on simplifying steps and assisting peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Keyword Matching & Gap-fill):</b> Students receive a handout with simple images (e.g., a paper bin, a plastic bottle) and a list of keywords (paper, plastic, bin, sort, recycle). They match the keyword to the image. Then, they complete simple sentences related to the project using these keywords: "We ______ our trash first." "There is a ______ for plastic bottles." "We ______ paper in this bin."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Frames for Explanations):</b> In pairs, students use sentence frames to explain one step of the "Recycling Data Chart Project" poster. For example: "First, we ______ our trash. We can ______ paper and plastic." or "For plastic, there is a ______ here. We ______ plastic bottles to help the environment." The teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pronunciation, especially /r/ in "recycle" and /s" in "sort."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Peer-Assisted Explanation):</b> Students work in new pairs. Partner A explains "Step 1: Sort your trash" from the project poster to Partner B, using gestures and pointing to visuals. Partner B listens and asks one clarifying question (e.g., "What is 'sort'?"). Partner A tries to simplify the explanation (e.g., "Sort means classify. Paper here, plastic here."). They then switch roles for "Step 2: Collect data every day." The focus is on assisting and simplifying.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Recycling for Our World".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in small groups (3-4 students) to mediate the "Recycling Data Chart" project. Each group receives a blank "Recycling Data Chart" template and pictures of different types of waste and recycling bins. Their task is to explain the steps of the project to each other, ensuring everyone understands how to collect data for the chart.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group explanation and peer assistance. Students take turns explaining a different step of the "Recycling Data Chart Project" to their group members, using the poster, the blank chart, and the waste/bin pictures. They use phrases like "First, we sort our waste. We can put paper here, plastic there," and "Look at this chart, we mark 'paper' here. We always count plastic bottles every week."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students practice asking for and giving clarification: "Could you clarify what to do with food scraps?" (Expected answer: "Food scraps are for compost, not the chart. Composting is easy!"). They use gestures and point to visuals to make their explanations clear. The teacher circulates, observing mediation strategies and providing support as needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in explaining procedures, collaborative interaction, using target vocabulary (e.g., plastic, bottles, waste, sort, classify, environment) and grammar (present simple, can/could, there is/are), and integrating frequency adverbs (always, every week) to describe data collection habits.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher observes students during the group activity, noting their ability to explain, clarify, and assist peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student, individually, selects one step from the "Recycling Data Chart Project" poster and explains it to the teacher, using keywords, pointing to the visual, and demonstrating their ability to simplify the instruction. The teacher might ask a clarifying question to prompt further mediation (e.g., "What if someone doesn't understand 'sort'?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially /r/ in "recycle" and /s/ in "sort" or "sustainability").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., plastic, paper, bin, sort, classify, recycle, help, environment).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target grammar (present simple, can/could, there is/are).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation and simplification of steps for a peer.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of gestures and pointing to visuals to assist understanding.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer on a small piece of paper: <br>1. "Can I explain how to sort trash for our project?" (Yes/No/A little) <br>2. "Can I help a friend understand the steps for our recycling chart?" (Yes/No/A little)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to explain about our recycling project? What was difficult?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can we help the environment by sharing what we learn about recycling?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this mediation lesson? Students were very engaged in explaining the project steps using visuals and helping each other. The peer interaction activities were effective for practicing simplification.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next time? Some students struggled to simplify their explanations beyond just repeating the instructions. I need to explicitly teach more strategies for simplification (e.g., using simpler synonyms, asking "Do you understand?"). More targeted practice on asking and answering "What is...?" questions to clarify vocabulary.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concluded the "Recycling for Our World" theme by launching the project. The next theme will build on environmental actions, perhaps focusing on "Saving Water," where students will need to explain simple steps for water conservation, continuing to practice mediation skills by explaining to others how to "save water."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>